\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The square-integrable range forces a small ratio}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{The condition #{0<p\lambda<1} forces #{\lambda<1/p}, hence
#{\lambda<1}. The prefactor in \ref{fdip-00FI} is therefore
positive on the whole range where the continuous density
exists. When #{\lambda=1}, \ref{fdip-00C5} gives increment
variance #{2} for every pair of distinct sites, and the
scaling in \ref{fdip-00FA} multiplies that variance by
#{p^k}. The product #{2p^k} is unbounded in #{k}. When
#{\lambda>1}, the product #{p\lambda} exceeds #{1}, so the
point variance of \ref{fdip-00FD} is unbounded. In every
parameter range the projective law of \ref{fdip-00C3} remains,
and the finite-depth ordering of \ref{fdip-00C5} is
unchanged.}
